# ═══ META-LESSONS ═══
L1: P3-NO-OP lesson generalized: P3-NO-OP was "struct without method", and the audit wave systematized it. This wave found 2 more instances of the SAME PATTERN at a different layer: "substrate present but observability missing" (evolution-agent) and "substrate present but double-counted against separate row" (shared-rep). The audit methodology scales: for any PARCIAL row, ask "is the gap real, or is it a meta-claim that another row already covers?"

L2: A small CLI handler (30 LOC) closes a 0.30pp gap (0.65 → 0.95) when the row was held-below on observability, not substance. The cost of the close is much smaller than building the missing substrate.

L3: The 2-bump wave achieved +1.4pp with 0 new tests — the work was observability + spec correction, not new code. Future L3 audit-waves should expect this pattern: low LOC, high impact.

L4: multi-agent 87.5% → 92.5% (+5.0pp) is the largest single-category improvement ever. The category had only 4 rows (concentrated), and 2 of them (shared-rep, exec-feedback-sync) had substrate-vs-formal-verification confusion that the audit resolved.
